": ["/fractional-cto/", "/technical-due-diligence/", "/regions/"], "citations": [{"title": "DORA's software delivery metrics: the four keys", "url": "https://dora.dev/guides/dora-metrics-four-keys/", "publisher": "DORA"}, {"title": "DORA research", "url": "https://dora.dev/research/", "publisher": "DORA"}, {"title": "The SPACE of Developer Productivity", "url": "https://queue.acm.org/detail.cfm?id=3454124", "publisher": "ACM Queue (Forsgren, Storey, Maddila, Zimmermann, Houck, Butler)", "date": "2021"}, {"title": "Analyzing changes to a repository's content", "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/viewing-activity-and-data-for-your-repository/analyzing-changes-to-a-repositorys-content", "publisher": "GitHub Docs"}]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>The question behind the question</h2>
<p>When a board asks how engineering is doing, it is rarely asking about engineering. It wants to know three things: are we spending this money well, are we exposed to something that could hurt us, and will the plan we approved actually ship? Engineering metrics are only useful in a board pack if they answer one of those.</p>
<p>The trouble is that the easiest numbers to produce answer none of them. Commit counts, story points, tickets closed and lines of code all measure activity. Repository activity graphs, such as commit activity over time<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, are genuinely useful to a team understanding its own rhythm. In a board pack, they invite the wrong conversation: why was a week quiet, why did a spike happen, can we get more of the busy weeks.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/engineering-metrics-for-boards/commit-activity.webp" alt="Bar chart of weekly commit counts over a year and a line chart of commits by day of the week, from a repository's commit activity graphs" width="882" height="593" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Commit activity: useful for a team, misleading for a board, because it measures effort rather than outcomes. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/viewing-activity-and-data-for-your-repository/analyzing-changes-to-a-repositorys-content" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Docs</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The researchers behind the SPACE framework make the underlying point well: productivity has several dimensions, including satisfaction, performance, activity, communication and efficiency, and no single metric, least of all an activity count, captures it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Activity numbers are also the easiest to game, and anything reported to a board will be optimised.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I put in front of boards</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Money</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Investment allocation<em>new product, maintenance, risk, platform</em></span><span>Cost to serve<em>cloud and AI spend per customer or transaction</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Delivery</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Speed and stability<em>DORA's four keys, as trends</em></span><span>Commitments<em>status of the few initiatives the board approved</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Risk</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Top technical risks<em>owner, impact, date</em></span><span>Reliability<em>against customer-facing targets</em></span><span>Security posture<em>exposures closed and open</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>People</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Key-person dependencies<em>where one departure would hurt</em></span><span>Hiring against plan<em>roles that block the roadmap</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Four sections, each answering a question the board actually has.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Where the money goes.</strong> The single most useful engineering chart for a board is how engineering time splits between new product, keeping existing systems running, reducing risk and platform work. It turns "why is the roadmap slow?" into a decision about allocation, which is the board's job.</p>
<p><strong>Speed and stability, as trends.</strong> DORA's four keys, deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate and time to restore service, describe delivery without counting individual effort<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. DORA's research ties them to organisational performance<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, which is why they belong in front of a board. Show quarter-on-quarter trends and explain the movements; absolute numbers mean little across companies.</p>
<p><strong>The top technical risks.</strong> Five at most, each with an owner, a plain-language impact and a date. Unsupported software, a single engineer who understands billing, an unrehearsed disaster recovery plan. Boards are good at risk; give them engineering risk in the form they already use.</p>
<p><strong>Commitments.</strong> The status of the handful of initiatives the board approved, with honest confidence levels. Not the whole roadmap.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I leave out, and why</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Why it stays out of the board pack</th><th>Where it belongs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Commits, lines of code</td><td>Measures activity, easily inflated</td><td>Nowhere as a target</td></tr>
<tr><td>Story points and velocity</td><td>Team-specific units, not comparable or stable</td><td>Team planning only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tickets closed</td><td>Rewards splitting work, ignores value</td><td>Team retrospectives</td></tr>
<tr><td>Individual productivity rankings</td><td>Damages collaboration and trust</td><td>Nowhere</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI tool usage counts</td><td>Adoption is not impact</td><td>Engineering leadership, alongside outcome measures</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>Every number in a board pack becomes a target. Before adding one, ask what behaviour you would get if the team optimised it perfectly.</p></div>
<p>AI coding tools have made this more urgent. Usage dashboards are easy to produce and look like progress. The question a board should ask is whether lead time, failure rate and allocation have moved, not how many suggestions were accepted. I cover the measurement side in <a href="/insights/dora-metrics-ai-assisted-engineering/">DORA metrics for AI-assisted engineering</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How to introduce this without a reporting project</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Quarter 1</small><b>Start simple</b><ul><li>Allocation from a time estimate</li><li>Top five risks</li><li>Status of approved initiatives</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Quarter 2</small><b>Add delivery</b><ul><li>DORA keys from CI and incidents</li><li>Explain each movement</li><li>Reliability against targets</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Quarter 3</small><b>Add cost</b><ul><li>Cloud and AI cost per unit</li><li>Trend against growth</li><li>Decisions it informed</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Ongoing</small><b>Keep it stable</b><ul><li>Same format every quarter</li><li>Retire what nobody uses</li><li>One page, with appendix</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Build the board view over three quarters, starting with what needs no tooling.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Consistency matters more than sophistication. A board that sees the same one-page view every quarter learns to read it and starts asking better questions. A different dashboard each time teaches it nothing.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The CTO's take</h2>
<p>Good engineering reporting makes the board a better partner: it can see trade-offs, back risk reduction and hold leadership to outcomes rather than activity. Bad reporting does the opposite, inviting micromanagement of numbers that do not matter. If you are a founder or CTO preparing a board pack, start with allocation, risk and commitments, add DORA trends, and leave the activity charts with the teams. Related reading: the <a href="/insights/board-ai-briefing-checklist/">board AI briefing checklist</a>, <a href="/insights/slos-error-budgets-small-teams/">SLOs for small teams</a> and <a href="/insights/technical-debt-without-a-rewrite/">technical debt without a rewrite</a>.</p>
<p>If you want help building an engineering board pack that leads to better decisions, I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. You can <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
